{
  "dataset": "Parametric Gaussian model of a representative Bayer RGB sensitivity (derived)",
  "ev": "derived",
  "calc": "Constrained least-squares Gaussian fit to the Nikon D700 R/G/B curves in camera-sensitivity-jiang2013.json (Jiang et al. 2013, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0), on the source's own 400-720 nm, 10 nm grid. For each channel, peak_nm and sigma_nm minimize the squared error against the measured curve, with the amplitude fixed so the Gaussian's photon-weighted response under CIE D65 (sum of S(lambda) * D65(lambda) * lambda) equals the measured channel's. The three amplitudes are then divided by the largest (G) so G peaks at 1.0. Result: R/G = 0.550 and B/G = 0.723 under D65, matching the measured D700 (0.550, 0.723); pinned in src/engine/data.test.ts. Refit 10/11/2026: the earlier method-of-moments fit (R sigma 48.6 nm, inflated by the red channel's secondary blue-green lobe, at the measured peak height) gave R/G = 0.955, about 1.74x too much red under daylight. Only the three fitted numbers per channel are our own computed statistics of that measured curve, not a reproduction of the table; provided as an unencumbered stand-in wherever the NC-SA restriction on the raw Jiang data is a problem. It reproduces each channel's integrated daylight response, peak wavelength and rough width, but not its fine structure (the red channel's sharp 580 nm edge and secondary lobe, CFA crosstalk ripple) -- label any use of it 'representative CFA shape, not a measured camera' in the UI.",
